course: everyday
lesson: 427
title: «I did brush my teeth»: little lies at home
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Did you brush your teeth?» «Yeah.» Owen's mum in Cardiff touched the toothbrush: bone dry. «Tamping I am, Owen» — in Wales «tamping» means furious — «back up those stairs.» The tablet went off early for the lie. So Owen got clever and wet the brush under the tap without brushing. It worked for a week. Then the dentist said «Somebody hasn't been brushing» and showed the brown bits on his back teeth. You can trick a mum, but not your own teeth.
explain: Little lies at home are about easy jobs — hands, teeth, showers, tidying, reading — and they are usually caught by something small. Mia in Melbourne said she'd washed her hands while holding a sandwich in grey sandpit fingers; Dad said «Yeah nah, mate», which in Australia means «no». Aisha said she'd read her chapter about a dragon; the book was about a pony. Tom in Newcastle came down «showered» with dry hair and the same socks, and his sister said «Howay man, you smell like a PE bag.» Next time Tom just asked «can I go after this game?», and his mum said yes.
explain: Some little lies are not little at all. Sam told Dad he'd fed the hamster — he hadn't, for two days. The hamster was fine after food, but Dad was properly angry: «You can lie about your teeth, but not about an animal that can't tell me.» Liam in Texas said he took his cough medicine, but Grandma checked the line on the bottle. «Boy, you fixin' to lie to me?» — «fixin' to» means «about to». Lies about animals and medicine can hurt somebody.
explain: Jess «tidied» by stuffing everything into the wardrobe: when Mum opened it, an avalanche of socks, Lego and a mouldy apple fell on her, and the proper sorting took three times longer. And Grace hid her broccoli in her napkin and said «All gone!» Nobody ever found out. She got pudding — and that night, when Dad praised her for «eating like a champion», it felt worse than being caught.
fact: Lies about easy jobs at home are usually caught by small clues: a dry toothbrush, dry hair, dirty hands.
fact: Tricking a check, like wetting a toothbrush, fools a person but not your body: teeth that are not brushed get decay.
fact: Dentists can see when teeth have not been brushed well, from plaque and early decay.
fact: Lying about feeding a pet can hurt the animal, because nobody else knows it is hungry.
fact: Lying about taking medicine can keep you ill longer; if it tastes awful, say so, because there may be another kind.
fact: If you cannot say what happened in a chapter, people know you did not read it.
fact: A half-truth, like a clear floor with everything stuffed in the wardrobe, is still a trick.
fact: A job hidden badly usually takes longer to fix than the job done properly.
fact: Asking honestly for «five more minutes» often gets you more than a lie does.
fact: Getting away with a lie does not always feel like winning; guilt can feel worse than being caught.
fact: Each small lie teaches people not to trust your «yes».
fact: «Tamping» (Wales) means furious; «yeah nah» (Australia, New Zealand) means no; «howay» (North East England) means «come on».
exercise: How did Owen's mum know he hadn't brushed? | The toothbrush was bone dry | She heard no tap running in the bathroom | His breath smelled strongly of mints | His little sister told on him at bedtime
exercise: What was Owen's wet-toothbrush trick really fooling? | Only Mum — not his teeth | His teeth and the dentist as well | Nobody, it was obvious at once | The toothbrush itself, which stayed wet
exercise: How did the trick finally come out? | The dentist found decay on his back teeth | Mum saw the tap running while he played on his phone | The toothbrush went mouldy in the cup | Owen told her by accident
exercise: Mia said she'd washed her hands. What gave her away? | Her hands were still grey from the sandpit | She had a clean towel, still folded on the rail | Her hands smelled of soap | She was wearing gloves
exercise: Why was Dad so angry about Sam and the hamster? | The animal couldn't tell anyone it was hungry | Hamsters are very expensive to buy and to keep | Sam fed it too much food | The hamster bit Dad
exercise: How did Mum find out Aisha hadn't read? | Aisha couldn't say what happened in the chapter | The book still had its bookmark on the very first page | Aisha's teacher rang home | The reading log was blank
exercise: How did Liam's grandma know he hadn't taken his medicine? | The bottle was still at the same mark | His cough sounded much louder in the night | The spoon was clean | Liam told her the truth
exercise: Why is lying about medicine dangerous? | It can keep you ill for longer | Medicine works better if you lie about it | It makes the medicine in the bottle go off | It is not dangerous at all
exercise: Jess's floor was clear. Why was it still a trick? | Everything was stuffed in the wardrobe | She had tidied it perfectly, every last corner | Her mum had done it for her | The floor was never messy
exercise: What happened after the wardrobe avalanche? | Jess had to sort it properly, which took longer | Mum tidied it all for her | Jess was allowed out at once | Mum shut the wardrobe and forgot
exercise: How was Tom caught out after his «shower»? | Dry hair and the same socks | The shower was still running when he came down | He was wrapped in a big towel, dripping | The soap was missing from the shower
exercise: What did Tom do next time instead of lying? | Asked «can I go after this game?» | Wet his hair in the sink to fool Mum | Hid his socks under the bed | Showered twice to be sure
exercise: Grace got away with hiding her broccoli. How did she feel? | Worse when Dad praised her | Proud of her clever little trick | Hungry for some more broccoli | Nothing at all, she forgot it
exercise: What might Grace have done instead? | Told Dad she hates broccoli and asked for peas | Fed the broccoli to the dog | Hidden it in her pocket | Eaten it all and cried
exercise: What does a string of small lies do over time? | Teaches people not to trust your «yes» | Makes people trust you more and more each time | Nothing, small lies don't count | Makes jobs get done faster
test: Mum in Cardiff: «Tamping I am!» How is she feeling? | Furious | Sleepy | Happy | Hungry
test: Kid: «just wet the brush, mum never knows lol». What is wrong with this plan? | Your teeth still decay and the dentist will see | Mum always smells the brush to check it's been used | Wet brushes break | It's a great plan with no problem
test: Dad: «Yeah nah, mate» after looking at Mia's hands. What does he mean? | No, they're not washed | Yes, they're clean enough | Maybe, I'm not sure | Hurry up and eat
test: «I fed him!!» says Sam, but the bowl is empty and the hamster chews its bedding. What would have been the right thing? | Say «I forgot» so someone could feed him | Fill the bowl quietly and say nothing at all | Blame the hamster for eating fast | Buy a bigger bowl
test: Grandma in Texas: «Boy, you fixin' to lie to me?» What does «fixin' to» mean? | About to | Trying to fix | Never going to | Always going to
test: The medicine tastes horrible. What's the smart move? | Tell the grown-up so they can try another kind | Pretend to take it | Pour it down the sink | Say you're better already
test: Big sister in Newcastle: «Howay man, you smell like a PE bag.» Is she being kind? | No, she's blunt but right | Yes, she loves the smell | She's asking for his PE bag | She wants to go out
test: What would happen if Jess had tidied properly from the start? | It would have taken less time than the avalanche | She'd have needed twice as long | Mum would still find socks falling | The apple would still be mouldy
test: Grace: «nobody found out so it doesn't matter». Which is TRUE? | She still felt bad, especially when praised | Nobody finding out means it was the right thing | Broccoli doesn't count | She felt great all night
test: Mum: «what happened in the chapter?» Aisha: «um, stuff... with a dragon». The book is about a pony. What should Aisha do? | Admit it and read the chapter | Say the pony turns into a dragon | Say she read a different book | Say Mum is wrong
test: In a cartoon a kid hides all the mess in a cupboard and is never found out. What happens more often in real life? | The cupboard is opened and the mess falls out | The mess somehow vanishes on its own overnight | Parents never open cupboards | The cupboard tidies itself
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the wetting of a toothbrush without brushing fails to conceal neglect. | The condition of the teeth is subsequently revealed upon dental examination | Wet toothbrushes are prohibited | Parents invariably observe the procedure | Toothbrushes record their use electronically
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a false statement that a pet has been fed. | It may cause harm to an animal unable to communicate its hunger | It is of no consequence provided the animal survives until the next meal | It is preferable to an admission of forgetfulness | It improves the health of the animal
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the concealment of untidiness within a wardrobe? | It is a half-truth, and proper correction subsequently requires more time | It constitutes complete tidying | It is wholly honest, the floor being clear | It shortens the total labour required
exam: It is given that a child successfully conceals uneaten food and receives praise. Determine the consequence most commonly reported. | A feeling of guilt more unpleasant than discovery | Lasting satisfaction at the success | An increased liking for the very food that was concealed | No feeling of any kind
